      "title": "Probe source availability and track field coverage",
      "content": "When prior run evidence reports uniform fetch failures on a host, test one or two returned URLs before committing another large batch to that host. Recheck availability rather than assuming a permanent outage. After a uniformly failed batch, inspect saved search hits for authoritative alternative hosts and probe one or two URLs before expanding fetching or repeating discovery searches. Preserve search results and successful fetched text, track failed URLs, and avoid unchanged failed batches. Changing to same-host PDFs does not establish recovery; ordinary URLs and unusual suffixes both failed, so the cause remains unknown. Treat FETCH_FAILED as unavailable evidence, never citation text. Track field coverage separately from fetch status: a successful retrieval can explicitly report that a requested field is absent. Consult those absence notes before another focused fetch; seek a different source unless there is a concrete reason to believe the earlier retrieval omitted relevant material. Preserve each successful retrieval separately, including its focus and field limitations, so later failures or narrower results do not overwrite usable evidence. Expand only after inspecting probe results; proposed fallback calls are not evidence of recovery.",
      "module": null
    },
